{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>Hear now My words.</b> One must know why it was necessary to say thus — surely they would hear what He would speak. Further, one must examine His saying the word <b>na</b>, for it is not fitting for a master to speak to his servant in the language of request." "Indeed, HaShem in the pleasantness of His words intends to make them feel the withholding of the hearing beforehand — for now they are fit to hear, but not before, on account of the impurity of their — of blessed memory — words against him. And from the inference of the matter you know that it is not proper for a servant to be deprived of preparation at the time his master commands him, and 'the ear tests words' (Job 12:11), for Moses was compelled by his deeds to separate from his wife. And by this approach we have found an answer to the first question: why did HaShem not give them the reason for the separation in His words to them, since that is the main thing that needed to be made heard by them — and according to this, HaShem gave the utterance that proclaims a great host." "<b>Further,</b> He may intend along this line: <b>Hear now</b> — meaning, now you are hearing for the purpose of disciplining you and reproving you, and it is not fitting to speak these things to you through Moses for two reasons: first, because he is involved in the matter and the words concern him; and further, for the reason stated in the matter itself, that HaShem called Aaron and Miriam to speak with them not in Moses' presence — but beforehand and afterward I do not speak with you, and this is a contradiction to their statement of blessed memory that He spoke also with them. And you will find that they of blessed memory said (Numbers Rabbah 14:19) that in every place where Scripture says ״וַיְדַבֵּר ה׳ אֶל מֹשֶׁה וְאֶל אַהֲרֹן״ the intention of the speech is not also with Aaron but rather with Moses, that he should tell Aaron — and these are the things that HaShem alluded to in the word ״נָא״." "<b>And He was precise</b> to say <b>My words</b>, and did not suffice to say ״שִׁמְעוּ נָא״ — since it is understood that what they would hear are His words — He intended this: that even though they had prophesied beforehand, it was not in such an order that they would hear the words from the mouth of El Elyon, as HaShem explained His words immediately afterward: ״אִם יִהְיֶה נְבִיאֲכֶם ה׳״ etc." "<b>Further,</b> it is possible that He alluded to them that He would not speak with them again but only now, so they would not think in their minds that they had been prepared for prophecy as exalted as this, and that it would be likewise the next day — therefore He said 'now.'" "It further intends in the word <b>na</b>: because they were impure with the impurity of keri, and in the laws of purification from impurity the Torah stated (Leviticus 22:6) that immersion does not complete purification for eating terumah until sunset, and the matter of prophecy — since we find that the bestowal of the holy spirit is required in order for him to be fit for HaShem to speak with him — water purification should not suffice until the sun comes and he is purified, for this should not be inferior to the purification required for eating terumah. And here we find that on the very day they were impure with the impurity of keri, close to their immersion, HaShem spoke with them. Therefore HaShem said <b>שִׁמְעוּ נָא דְבָרָי</b> — meaning, now specifically is it a provisional ruling (הוראת שעה) that I make you hear My words, even though you still require sunset, in order to fulfill what is said (Genesis Rabbah 55:11): 'love disrupts the regimen' — the love of HaShem for Moses His beloved did not allow Him to wait until the next day." "<b>If he be your prophet, etc.</b> And He did not speak to them directly, 'If you will be prophets,' because in their argument they mentioned other prophets besides themselves, and as the midrash says (Sifrei) that they said: Did He not also speak with Abraham, and it is not specified, etc. Therefore His word, may He be blessed, came to make a distinction also from what was hinted in their words, to Moses. And perhaps it is for this reason that He used the singular form, 'if he be.'" "<b>YHWH in a vision to him, etc.</b> He comes to inform us of the difference between the level of the prophets and the level of Moses. And since there are three matters in the subject of prophecy: first, the grasp of the vision; second, the time of grasping it — meaning, whether he prophesies at any time he wishes, or the matter does not depend on his will but on the time that YHWH desires; third, the grasp of the aspect of speech, as I will explain." "And YHWH began to speak regarding the aspect of the level of grasping the vision, and YHWH said <b>in a vision to him I make Myself known</b>, meaning, because there are three general levels in the knowledge of HaShem: The first, no person has attained it through prophecy, and it is what Moses asked (Exodus 33:18), ״הַרְאֵנִי נָא אֶת כְּבוֹדֶךָ״ — meaning, 'na' — now: that which You are destined to show me after death, for Moses knew what is grasped by the righteous in the supernal world. And HaShem answered him, ״לֹא תוּכַל וְגוֹ׳ כִּי לֹא יִרְאַנִי הָאָדָם וָחָי״ — except after death. And this level is in the very essence of His light, may He be blessed, and even though the term 'essence' should not be applied to Him except to soften the ear, etc. The second level is the light emanating from Him, may He be blessed, to which the term 'form' (תמונה) may be applied, and this form is unlike any form of existing things in the created order, for it has no form but a form hidden from the eyes of all living, which none knows but Moses, to whom HaShem made it known, as it is written, ״וּתְמֻנַת ה׳ יַבִּיט״ — what none other than he attained among all the prophets. The third level is the glimmer of His light, may He be blessed, from a distance. To what may this be compared — to a burning lamp whose light travels far and a person sees that light, and this is called 'mar'eh' (vision), and this is the grasp of the prophets other than Moses, and it is the statement of HaShem, 'בַּמַּרְאָה' — understand this; and corresponding to it He said of Moses, ״וּתְמֻנַת ה׳ יַבִּיט״." "<b>And corresponding</b> to the time of grasping prophecy, He said <b>to him I make Myself known</b> — meaning, even a small level such as this is not his constantly, at whatever time he wishes to attain it; rather, when I wish, I make Myself known to him in it, and when he wishes to attain it, he is not assured that he will attain it at all times, as Moses is assured at all times whenever he wishes, as it is written (Numbers 9:8), ״עִמְדוּ וְאֶשְׁמְעָה מַה יְצַוֶּה ה׳״ — without any doubt in the matter. You thus find that all the prophets — prophecy is not assured to them, even though it is a lesser level than the level of Moses; and Moses — his prophecy, even though it is greater than theirs, is assured to him at all times whenever he wishes. And this is the meaning of <b>in all My house he is trusted</b> — meaning, like this trusted one, in whose hand and authority is everything to do all that he wishes, so too is Moses. And He was precise to say <b>in all My house</b>, to include that even in the attainment of great prophecy, the matter is in his authority to prophesy — which would not have been understood had He said merely 'in My house he is trusted,' for I would have said that the matter is not given to his will but only the lesser level shared by all the prophets; and the difference is that they — the matter is not in their authority, unlike him. Hence it was necessary to say 'in all,' etc." "<b>And corresponding to</b> the grasp of the dimension of speech, He said <b>in a dream I speak with him</b> — meaning, know that there are two characteristics in the communication of dreams: the first — when he hears the words, his mind is befuddled and he cannot discern the meaning of things to understand what is spoken, for his mind is confused; the second — everything spoken in a dream is not according to its plain sense but rather parable and imagery. Go out and learn from the dreams written in the Torah and their interpretations. This is what He said — for the prophets, when His good word comes to them, their mind becomes confused and they are like a person lying in the heart of the sea, and they are driven to frenzy; moreover, the speech does not come to them in its form but rather clothed within imagery and parable, so that they can endure the speech — and even so they are driven to frenzy. And the meaning of the word <b>in a dream</b> is in the category of a dream in quality and essence, and always while awake YHWH would reveal Himself to His servants the prophets; but when the word came to them they would receive it with the character of a dream as mentioned, and they would fall to the ground and their mind would be deranged from them — unlike Moses, who was free of this, for his mind did not change when the word of YHWH came, and he would hear with a complete mind; moreover, the words did not come to him by way of parable and allegory but rather explicit and clear words requiring no interpretation. And it is His statement <b>mouth to mouth I speak with him</b> — meaning: the eyes of all Israel see the fifty-three sections that YHWH spoke to Moses, all of which are words whose plain sense is understood by anyone who understands the holy tongue. Also, the treasures of wisdom that YHWH stored within them are all present to the discerning person who comes to examine and probe deeply; he will sense in the meaning of the verses themselves what YHWH intended to say through them. And this is not the manner of the words that YHWH spoke through His servants the prophets Jeremiah and Isaiah, which are all parable and allegory, as His statement (Ezekiel 17:3): ״הַנֶּשֶׁר הַגָּדוֹל בַּעַל הַכְּנָפַיִם״ etc., and even more so than these, the prophecy of Zechariah, who locked the gates of prophecy — for he locked even his own prophecy, and we do not have with us anyone who knows the intent of his prophecy." "<b>And there is</b> to investigate this: since the One who prophesies is YHWH our Elohim, one, why to this one does He speak directly and to that one His words are convoluted? And the men of truth have said, in what has preceded us as knowledge regarding the matter of the angel to Daniel — the sensation that it produced in him — from this you will gain insight to see that the infusion of the human species nullifies the sensation of spirituality, and it cannot endure other than through the composite that YHWH established with His wondrous wisdom, so that soul and body can be joined together. And surely you will see that even among physical forces that are perceptible and grasped — fire and water — one consumes the other, except for the heavens that the hands of the Creator established. And from this you will apprehend how one can stand in the word of Elohim, the source of spiritualities, as He hews His voice into a flame of fire — how can one stand? Therefore no person is able to endure in his own strength to hear the voice of the living Elohim, except for the man who transformed his material nature and made it into a form, as we have written in other places — such a one has no body and can endure the word of the King. Therefore, when YHWH prophesies to a man whose body does not resemble that form, YHWH acts wisely in this manner: the word of the King goes forth from the mouth of the Most High, and the speech is cloaked in subtle airs, and afterward it reaches the ear of the prophet — and by this the prophet can endure it. Yet even this produces a sensation in the prophets driving them to frenzy, and this results in the words coming by way of parable and allegory in the manner of a dream. Therefore all the prophets except Moses spoke parables and allegories, and more than all of them Zechariah, who was the last in prophecy — the speech came to him wrapped and concealed more than all other prophecies, so that he could endure it; and therefore the allegories and depth of imagery multiplied in him to the point that later generations do not know his words." "And this is what YHWH informed here, in the dimension of what is grasped, to Moses, and He said <b>mouth to mouth</b> — meaning, the speech was not interrupted from the mouth of the Most High on its way to Moses to pass through the air of the world, such that the speech would change through the donning of airs and turn to speak words of parables and riddles; He also removed the sensory upheaval that befalls the prophets, as stated <b>and the appearance</b> — meaning, he would not fall asleep nor be alarmed, but rather ״וּמַרְאֶה״ — meaning, at the very time and hour that I speak with him mouth to mouth, it is not in the manner of a dream, but rather ״וּמַרְאֶה״ — that I show him the light of the prophetic source and he gazes; and this declares the lucidity and attunement of the intellect." "And His saying <b>and not in riddles</b> comes to alert us regarding the consequence of the matter, that since He would speak mouth to mouth, on this account as well the speech went forth without riddles. And He was precise to say <b>to the mouth</b> and not to the ear — one who reflects on the matter of the prophet who said (I Kings 3:9) ״לֵב שׁוֹמֵעַ״ (״a hearing heart״) will know the secret of the matter. And this is the level of prophecy than which there is none higher: that YHWH binds the soul of the prophet to the prophetic source and causes the prophecy to descend mouth to mouth. And through this you will also understand His saying <b>I speak with him</b> — and He did not say ״with him״ (אִתּוֹ / עִמּוֹ) but rather בּוֹ (״in him״) — and understand. Further, He intends to give you insight that the speech came only from the mouth of YHWH to Moses, and he did not look upon the face of any created being — and this is the meaning of ״בּוֹ״ (״in him״)."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:02.479223894Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}